---
scope: cli
status: aligned
description: >
  Terminal-native two-panel TUI for humans to browse and inspect the .aide intent tree of any project without opening individual files or running MCP tools.
intent: >
  Give developers a terminal-native way to explore and inspect the .aide intent
  tree of any project — a single command that renders the full hierarchy as a
  navigable, searchable TUI so a human can understand the intent structure at a
  glance without opening individual files or running MCP tools manually. The CLI
  tree view exists to show humans what agents see via aide_discover — it is a
  debugging and inspection tool for verifying the intent tree as agents experience
  it. The TUI should render the same information aide_discover returns
  (descriptions, status flags, ancestor chains) so a developer can visually
  confirm what any agent would see when it calls the tool.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - A two-panel layout: navigable intent tree on the left, detail pane on the right showing contextual info for the selected node. The tree is always visible and navigable — it is never hidden or replaced.
    - Tree supports keyboard navigation (arrow keys, enter to drill in, escape/backspace to go back), search/filter by typing, and a toggle between shallow (locations + types only) and deep (with summaries) views.
    - Drilling into any .aide file renders a formatted inspection view in the right panel — frontmatter displayed as a styled card with scope at top, intent below it, and desired/undesired outcomes displayed side-by-side in their own bordered regions. The tree on the left remains visible and active throughout.
    - Body sections (Context, Strategy, Good examples, Bad examples) listed below the frontmatter card as expandable items the user can cycle through with a keypress. For content too long to fit the panel, the user can open the raw file directly in their editor.
    - Ships as a launcher script written into the user's project during `aide_init` — `.aide/bin/aide-tree.mjs` is created by the CLI bootstrapper and the `installAideTree` MCP helper, resolving the installed `@aidemd-mcp/server` package's TUI entry point at runtime via `createRequire`. Users run the TUI as `node .aide/bin/aide-tree.mjs` from their project root.
    - The tree panel displays the frontmatter description field as the summary for each node — when a spec has a description in its frontmatter, that text takes precedence over body-derived summaries. Descriptions appear unconditionally in the tree, not gated behind the deep-view toggle. The tree panel is wide enough to show meaningful description text without truncating it to uselessness — at least 55-60% of terminal width so descriptions remain readable alongside the node path and type tag.
    - The detail panel renders plan and todo file types with their own layout — plan files show checklist items with completion progress, todo files show the issue list with misalignment annotations. The intent-card layout (scope, intent, outcomes) is reserved for intent and research file types only.
    - The tree renders the same information an agent receives from aide_discover — descriptions, alignment status flags, file types, and ancestor chain context — so a developer can visually verify what agents see without calling the MCP tool directly.
  undesired:
    - A static dump that requires re-running the command to see different parts of the tree — the whole point is persistent interactive navigation.
    - Frontmatter rendered as raw YAML — the nested outcomes structure must be visually parsed into labeled, bordered regions, not shown as indented key-value pairs.
    - Requiring the MCP server to be running — the TUI reads the filesystem directly, reusing the scan/classify logic but not the MCP transport.
    - A full CLI framework with subcommands, flags, and help text — this is one command, one view, zero arguments beyond an optional project root path.
    - A full-screen drill-in view that hides the tree panel when the user inspects a file. The dual-panel layout exists so the developer can see both the tree and the detail at the same time — a mode that takes over the screen defeats the entire purpose of the two-panel design.
    - A tree node that shows a truncated snippet of body text when the spec's frontmatter already contains a one-line description — the description field exists precisely to provide a human-readable summary, and ignoring it forces the tree to display less meaningful text.
    - Plan and todo files rendered through the same intent-card template as intent specs — checklist items displayed as unformatted markdown body text with no completion state, and frontmatter arrays like misalignment silently dropped because the renderer does not expect them.
    - A tree view that diverges from what aide_discover returns — if the CLI shows different summaries, omits status flags, or structures the hierarchy differently than the tool output, it fails as a debugging surface.
---

## Context

The MCP server gives agents a programmatic way to explore intent trees, but the human maintainer has no equivalent. Understanding a project's intent structure today means opening individual files, mentally reconstructing parent-child relationships, and reading raw YAML frontmatter. For a project with ten or twenty specs spread across nested modules, this is slow enough that most developers stop doing it and lose track of how their intent tree has grown.

A terminal-native explorer solves this by making the intent tree a first-class navigable artifact. The developer launches one command, sees the full hierarchy, drills into any node, reads the formatted intent and outcomes, and walks back out — all without leaving the terminal or parsing YAML mentally. The same scan and classify logic that the MCP server uses to answer agent queries powers the TUI, so the human and the agent always see the same tree.

This module ships inside the existing package rather than as a standalone install. Developers who adopt the MCP server get the explorer automatically. No second install step, no version drift between the server and the explorer, no separate release pipeline.

## Strategy

**Two-panel split with the tree as the permanent navigation surface.** The left panel shows the intent tree — directories as collapsible groups, specs as leaf nodes tagged by type. The right panel shows contextual detail for whatever node is currently highlighted. This split lets the developer scan the full tree structure while previewing content without losing context. The tree panel gets roughly 55-60% of terminal width to ensure descriptions are readable; the detail panel gets the rest. The tree stays visible at all times — there is no mode, state, or keypress that hides it.

**The right panel is the only detail surface.** When the user highlights a tree node, the right panel shows a summary preview. When the user presses Enter to drill in, the right panel transitions to show the formatted frontmatter card — scope, intent, and outcomes rendered as styled bordered regions. Body sections appear below the card as collapsed items. The transition happens entirely within the right panel; the left panel (tree) is unaffected and remains navigable. Pressing Escape or Backspace returns the right panel to the summary preview for the current selection.

**Body sections use a cycle-through model for long content.** Below the frontmatter card, body sections (Context, Strategy, Good examples, Bad examples) are listed as expandable items. The user cycles through them with a keypress (e.g., Tab or bracket keys), expanding one section at a time while collapsing the previous. Each section scrolls within the right panel if it exceeds the available height. For specs with very long body sections, a keypress opens the raw file directly in the user's editor — the TUI does not try to be a full document reader.

**Shallow view is the default; deep view is opt-in.** On launch the tree shows only file locations and type tags — the same information the MCP server's shallow scan returns. A single keypress toggles to deep view, which reads file content and adds truncated summaries next to each node. This mirrors the progressive disclosure the methodology itself teaches: show structure first, detail on demand. It also keeps startup instant for large projects because shallow scan does no file reads.

**Keyboard navigation follows terminal conventions, not custom bindings.** Arrow keys move the cursor. Enter drills into a node (showing detail in the right panel). Escape or backspace walks back up. A search mode activates on typing any letter, filtering the tree to nodes whose path or summary matches the input. Tab cycles through body sections in the detail panel. These are the bindings a developer expects from any tree-navigation TUI; custom keybindings would require documentation the single-command design explicitly rejects.

**Frontmatter renders as a structured card, not raw YAML.** The detail view for a single spec file presents frontmatter in a bordered card layout: scope at the top as a heading, intent as a wrapped paragraph below it, then desired and undesired outcomes side by side (or stacked on narrow terminals) in their own labeled bordered regions. Each outcome is a bullet in its region. This visual parsing is the core differentiator — the raw YAML with nested indentation is what the spec's undesired outcomes explicitly prohibit.

**Reuse the existing scan and classify utilities directly.** The scan walker and file classifier are pure functions that take a root path and return typed results. The TUI imports them as library code, not through the MCP transport layer. This means the TUI has zero dependency on the MCP server running, and the tree the human sees is structurally identical to the tree an agent sees through the discover tool. If scan or classify gain new capabilities (new file types, new skip directories), the TUI inherits them automatically.

**The buildTree formatter is not reused — the TUI builds its own component tree.** The existing buildTree helper produces a flat string with box-drawing characters, designed for plain-text MCP responses. The TUI needs a stateful component tree where each node tracks selection, expansion, and highlight state. The scan results feed directly into the component tree; the string formatter is bypassed entirely.

**Single entry point, no argument parsing framework.** The command accepts one optional positional argument: the project root path. If omitted, it defaults to the current working directory. No flags, no subcommands, no help text beyond a one-line usage hint on invalid input. The Ink runtime handles the rest. This keeps the surface area minimal and avoids the complexity budget that a CLI framework like Commander or Yargs would introduce for a single-view tool.

**Ink components follow a flat hierarchy.** The top-level app component owns the view state (summary preview vs. drill-in detail) and delegates to two presentational components: one for the tree panel and one for the detail panel. The tree panel component manages cursor position, expansion state, and search filter. The detail panel component receives the selected file's parsed content and renders either the summary preview or the full frontmatter card with body sections. No deeper nesting — Ink's layout model handles the rest with flexbox-style composition.

## Good examples

A developer runs the command in a project with six specs. The terminal shows:

```
┌─ Intent Tree ───────────────┬─ Detail ─────────────────────────────┐
│                              │                                      │
│  . /                         │  scope: .                            │
│  └── .aide [intent]          │                                      │
│                              │  This repository is the canonical    │
│  src/tools/discover/         │  home of the AIDE methodology —      │
│  └── .aide [intent]          │  Autonomous Intent-Driven            │
│                              │  Engineering...                      │
│  src/tools/init/             │                                      │
│  ├── intent.aide [intent]    │  ✓ desired (5)                       │
│  └── research.aide [research]│  ✗ undesired (6)                     │
│                              │                                      │
│  src/tools/read/             │                                      │
│  └── .aide [intent]          │                                      │
│                              │                                      │
│  src/cli/                    │                                      │
│  └── .aide [intent]          │                                      │
│                              │                                      │
│                              │                                      │
│  [↑↓] navigate  [enter]     │                                      │
│  drill in  [tab] deep view   │                                      │
└──────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────┘
```

The developer presses Enter on the root `.aide`. The right panel transitions to show the formatted drill-in view while the tree remains visible and navigable on the left:

```
┌─ Intent Tree ───────────────┬─ . / .aide ───────────────────────────┐
│                              │                                       │
│  . /                         │  scope: .                             │
│  └── .aide [intent]  ←       │                                       │
│                              │  This repository is the canonical     │
│  src/tools/discover/         │  home of the AIDE methodology —       │
│  └── .aide [intent]          │  Autonomous Intent-Driven Engineering │
│                              │  ...                                  │
│  src/tools/init/             │                                       │
│  ├── intent.aide [intent]    │  ┌─ Desired ────┬─ Undesired ──────┐ │
│  └── research.aide [research]│  │ • The canon-  │ • A rule, conv-  │ │
│                              │  │   ical AIDE   │   ention, or     │ │
│  src/tools/read/             │  │   methodology │   doctrine that  │ │
│  └── .aide [intent]          │  │   lives in    │   lives anywhere │ │
│                              │  │   this repo...│   other than...  │ │
│  src/cli/                    │  └───────────────┴──────────────────┘ │
│  └── .aide [intent]          │                                       │
│                              │  ▸ Context (2 paragraphs)             │
│                              │  ▸ Strategy (7 decisions)             │
│                              │  ▸ Good examples (4 scenarios)        │
│                              │  ▸ Bad examples (6 scenarios)         │
│  [↑↓] navigate  [esc] back  │                                       │
│  [tab] cycle sections        │  [e] open in editor                   │
└──────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────┘
```

The developer presses Tab to expand the Strategy section in the right panel. The frontmatter card scrolls up and the Strategy content appears:

```
┌─ Intent Tree ───────────────┬─ . / .aide ───────────────────────────┐
│                              │                                       │
│  . /                         │  ▸ Context (2 paragraphs)             │
│  └── .aide [intent]  ←       │  ▾ Strategy                           │
│                              │    Canonical docs are editable files, │
│  src/tools/discover/         │    not compiled output. The doc set   │
│  └── .aide [intent]          │    lives in this repo as plain        │
│                              │    Markdown the maintainer can read,  │
│  src/tools/init/             │    diff, and edit. Every downstream   │
│  ├── intent.aide [intent]    │    artifact — tool descriptions,      │
│  └── research.aide [research]│    methodology blocks, slash command  │
│                              │    templates — is composed from those │
│  src/tools/read/             │    files at build or init time...     │
│  └── .aide [intent]          │                                       │
│                              │    Two delivery channels, no third... │
│  src/cli/                    │                                       │
│  └── .aide [intent]          │  ▸ Good examples (4 scenarios)        │
│                              │  ▸ Bad examples (6 scenarios)         │
│                              │                                       │
│  [↑↓] navigate  [esc] back  │  [tab] next section  [↑↓] scroll      │
│  [tab] cycle sections        │  [e] open in editor                   │
└──────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────┘
```

While in drill-in view, the developer uses arrow keys on the left panel to highlight a different file. The right panel updates to show that file's content, staying in drill-in mode. Navigation between specs never leaves the two-panel layout.

The developer toggles to deep view with Tab (when not in drill-in mode). Each tree node now shows a truncated summary:

```
│  ./                                                                  │
│  └── .aide [intent] — This repository is the canonical home of...    │
│                                                                      │
│  src/tools/discover/                                                 │
│  └── .aide [intent] — Scan for .aide files and return a progre...    │
```

The developer types "init" and the tree filters to show only nodes whose path contains "init":

```
│  src/tools/init/                                                     │
│  ├── intent.aide [intent]                                            │
│  └── research.aide [research]                                        │
```

## Bad examples

A full-screen drill-in view that hides the tree when inspecting a file:

```
┌─ . / .aide ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                                                                      │
│  scope: .                                                            │
│                                                                      │
│  This repository is the canonical home of the AIDE methodology —     │
│  Autonomous Intent-Driven Engineering, with a deliberate second      │
│  reading as AI Domain Expert — and the MCP server that delivers it   │
│  to agents in host projects.                                         │
│                                                                      │
│  ┌─ Desired Outcomes ──────────┬─ Undesired Outcomes ──────────────┐ │
│  │                              │                                    │ │
│  │  • The canonical AIDE        │  • A rule, convention, or          │ │
│  │    methodology lives in      │    doctrine that lives anywhere    │ │
│  │    this repo as editable     │    other than the canonical doc    │ │
│  │    doc files...              │    files...                        │ │
│  │                              │                                    │ │
│  └──────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────┘ │
│                                                                      │
│  ▸ Context (2 paragraphs)                                            │
│  ▸ Strategy (7 decisions)                                            │
│                                                                      │
│  [esc] back  [↑↓] scroll  [enter] expand section                    │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

The tree is gone. The developer cannot see where they are in the hierarchy, cannot glance at sibling specs, and cannot navigate to a different file without pressing Escape first. This is the same workflow as opening each file individually — the dual-panel TUI was built specifically to avoid this mode.

A flat list of file paths with no hierarchy or grouping:

```
.aide - intent
src/tools/discover/.aide - intent
src/tools/init/intent.aide - intent
src/tools/init/research.aide - research
src/tools/read/.aide - intent
```

This is the MCP discover output pasted into a terminal. It shows the same information but destroys the spatial relationships. A developer cannot see at a glance which specs are siblings, which directories have multiple file types, or where the tree is deep versus shallow.

Raw YAML frontmatter dumped verbatim when drilling into a file:

```
---
scope: .
intent: >
  This repository is the canonical home of the AIDE methodology...
outcomes:
  desired:
    - The canonical AIDE methodology — spec, template, progressive
      disclosure, agent-readable code, automated QA — lives in this
      repo as editable doc files...
    - Two delivery channels, each with one clear job...
  undesired:
    - A rule, convention, or doctrine that lives anywhere other than...
---
```

The nested YAML indentation makes outcomes harder to read than the source file. The developer opened the TUI to avoid reading YAML; showing YAML defeats the purpose. Desired and undesired outcomes are visually indistinguishable except by their parent key name, buried in the nesting.

A static one-shot dump that prints the tree and exits:

```
$ aide-tree
./
└── .aide [intent]
src/tools/discover/
└── .aide [intent]
...
$
```

The developer wanted to explore. Instead they got a printout they have to re-run with different arguments to see a different part of the tree. This is the MCP discover tool rendered to stdout — useful for an agent, useless for a human who wants to browse.

A TUI that requires flags to configure its view:

```
$ aide-tree --deep --filter=init --panel=right --format=card
```

This turns a browse-and-explore tool into a query tool. The developer has to know what they want before they start, which is the opposite of exploration. Every flag is a decision pushed from runtime to launch time, where it does not belong.

A drill-in view that shows all body sections expanded by default, pushing outcomes below the fold:

```
┌─ .aide ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  scope: .                                                            │
│  This repository is the canonical home of...                         │
│                                                                      │
│  ## Context                                                          │
│  Before this repo existed, the AIDE methodology lived in a personal  │
│  Obsidian vault. That worked for one maintainer but could not ship:  │
│  every new project, every new contributor, every new agent framework  │
│  had to rediscover the rules from a vault they did not have access   │
│  to, and any CLAUDE.md snippet that tried to carry the rules forward │
│  went stale the moment the canonical vault notes changed.            │
│  ... (scrolls for 40 more lines before outcomes appear)              │
```

The developer drilled in to see what this spec is *for* — intent and outcomes. Instead they got a wall of prose that buries the contract below the fold. Body sections must be collapsed by default so the frontmatter card dominates the initial view.
